WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.478
Welcome to the Train-the-Trainer walkthrough for Advanced GitHub
Copilot: Agents, Skills, and Subagents.

00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:16.845
This deck follows the v2 conceptual arc—surfaces, runtime harnesses,
Markdown customization, guardrails, and safe parallelism—and lands

00:00:16.845 --> 00:00:20.453
directly in Chronicle's six 90-minute hands-on labs.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:27.700
Open with energy: learners move from prompting one generalist
assistant to directing an engineering team.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:36.516
Walk the room through the two-part structure: present the mental
model cleanly up front, then run six tightly timeboxed labs totaling

00:00:36.516 --> 00:00:37.919
exactly ninety minutes.

00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:45.835
Point out the hard clock gates: by minute forty, every learner has
their Orchestrator and four skilled workers wired up; by minute

00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:52.350
fifty-five, Foundry connectivity is verified; and the final
seventeen minutes protect browser verification.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:01:00.093
Anchor the five learning outcomes in Chronicle, our zero-package
Node twenty-two session explorer.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:03.320
Why does Chronicle resonate so well in class?

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:10.705
Because learners experience context growth firsthand while building
an app that solves session handoff!

00:01:10.705 --> 00:01:19.662
Stress the two invariants early: zero external npm packages in
package.json, and original chat history files remain strictly

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:20.384
read-only.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:24.014
Transition into Part One: the Agent Landscape.

00:01:24.014 --> 00:01:32.102
Your goal in the next seven slides is to clear up vocabulary
confusion around surfaces, harnesses, the Agent Host, permissions,

00:01:32.102 --> 00:01:36.050
and context budgets before anyone writes a customization file.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:41.169
Use this four-step progression to reframe the developer's job.

00:01:41.169 --> 00:01:46.493
Most attendees already use inline completions, Chat, and
single-agent mode.

00:01:46.493 --> 00:01:50.966
Today's leap is the rightmost box: directing specialist agents.

00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:58.776
Warn them early that more agents only speed you up when tasks have
clear boundaries and non-overlapping files.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:05.024
Position all five surfaces as one family sharing your repo's
customizations.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:13.659
Contrast IDE Chat—best when inspecting lines right beside
src/server.js—with VS Code's Agents Window for multi-session

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:23.319
oversight, Copilot CLI for terminal-first planning and scripting,
the standalone GitHub Copilot App built on the CLI engine, and the

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:26.538
Cloud coding agent on GitHub-hosted runners.

00:02:26.538 --> 00:02:30.100
Reassure learners: our labs only require VS Code.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.922
Give a concrete 30-second tour here.

00:02:33.922 --> 00:02:45.534
Show how code dash-dash-agents opens the VS Code Agents Window with
a session grid, Branch-versus-New-Worktree picker, and turn-level

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:46.669
Changes view.

00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:57.736
Then highlight how Copilot CLI and the desktop Copilot App pair
slash-plan with slash-fleet for parallel subagent execution and

00:02:57.736 --> 00:03:04.359
pull-request review—all reading the exact same dot-github skills and
agents.

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:11.914
Hammer home that Mode, Permissions, and Isolation are three separate
dials—never conflate them!

00:03:11.914 --> 00:03:16.383
Plan mode is a workflow conversation, NOT a security boundary.

00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:21.429
And a Git worktree isolates file edits, NOT machine or network
access.

00:03:21.429 --> 00:03:29.574
Because Chronicle labs use private Foundry credentials in dot-env,
forbid blanket Approve-All in local checkouts.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.699
This slide resolves one of the most common points of confusion in
advanced workshops.

00:03:36.699 --> 00:03:46.175
Separate three layers: the UI client window, the Harness
runtime—such as Copilot SDK, VS Code Local, Claude, or Codex—and the

00:03:46.175 --> 00:03:47.615
Agent Host process.

00:03:47.615 --> 00:03:54.165
Ask the room two check questions: Does switching from Sonnet to GPT
change the harness?

00:03:54.165 --> 00:03:55.595
No, only the model.

00:03:55.595 --> 00:03:57.025
Is Cloud a harness?

00:03:57.025 --> 00:03:59.510
No—Cloud is where execution runs!

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:06.888
And because Agent Host owns the session in its own process, closing
a window doesn't kill the run.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:15.445
Walk through the seven layers competing inside one context window
and point to the rising ten-turn bar chart.

00:04:15.445 --> 00:04:25.228
Every turn re-sends the accumulated conversation and tool outputs,
driving up token consumption and causing middle-of-context drift, or

00:04:25.228 --> 00:04:26.098
context rot.

00:04:26.098 --> 00:04:35.433
Connect this directly to BOTH halves of our labs: subagents keep
noisy exploration out of your main chat, and Chronicle's chunked

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:39.629
summarizer turns huge transcripts into lean handoff files.

00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:48.600
Present Research, Plan, Implement as the rhythm learners will see
their Chronicle Orchestrator execute automatically.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:57.636
Research reads files and surfaces risks; Plan produces an explicit
file contract; Implement writes a focused diff and runs node

00:04:57.636 --> 00:04:58.703
dash-dash-test.

00:04:58.703 --> 00:05:03.649
Each phase passes forward a compact summary instead of raw tool
noise.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:08.753
Welcome to Part Two: Controlling Agents and Skills in Markdown.

00:05:08.753 --> 00:05:17.926
Slow down slightly on the next five slides—learners will author
these exact YAML frontmatter fields in Labs One and Two, so clarity

00:05:17.926 --> 00:05:21.217
on syntax and enforcement pays off immediately.

00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:27.088
Walk this table top to bottom and spotlight the rightmost column:
Guarantee!

00:05:27.088 --> 00:05:33.329
Instructions and Skills are model-driven—prose guides behavior, it
doesn't enforce security.

00:05:33.329 --> 00:05:42.402
Custom Agent tool lists enforce capability boundaries, Subagents
enforce context isolation, Hooks enforce deterministic shell checks,

00:05:42.402 --> 00:05:45.199
and Canvases provide shared visual state.

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:48.183
Ask the room this distinction during recaps!

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.430
Break down the open Agent Skills format under dot-github slash
skills.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:06:01.773
Stress rule number one for Lab Two: the YAML name field must match
the parent folder name character-for-character—lowercase with

00:06:01.773 --> 00:06:02.294
hyphens!

00:06:02.294 --> 00:06:11.003
Highlight three-stage progressive disclosure on the right: metadata
costs roughly a hundred tokens at startup, the SKILL.md body loads

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:17.177
only when matched, and bundled scripts execute deterministically
without bloating prompt prose.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.819
The description field is the router trigger!

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:29.497
Contrast the red anti-pattern—'Helpful test utilities'—against our
actual Lab Two skill description that names local mock servers,

00:06:29.497 --> 00:06:31.833
OAuth2, and Foundry client changes.

00:06:31.833 --> 00:06:40.717
Coach learners to test skill discovery with natural user prompts
that do NOT mention the skill's filename—if it doesn't fire, sharpen

00:06:40.717 --> 00:06:41.785
the description!

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.990
Walk every YAML field in chronicle-security.agent.md.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:54.813
Point out tools: read and search—omitting edit and execute creates a
true read-only auditor enforced by the harness, not polite prose.

00:06:54.813 --> 00:07:02.842
Explain user-invocable: false—it hides specialist workers from the
user's Chat dropdown to prevent clutter, while keeping

00:07:02.842 --> 00:07:11.402
disable-model-invocation false so Chronicle Orchestrator can still
invoke them as subagents, preloaded with their matching skills

00:07:11.402 --> 00:07:11.800
array!

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.544
Keep this compatibility table handy during Labs One and Two!

00:07:16.544 --> 00:07:25.416
Notice why harness awareness matters: the Copilot harness, CLI,
Copilot App, and Cloud agent support skills, mcp-servers, and hooks

00:07:25.416 --> 00:07:30.969
frontmatter, whereas VS Code's Local harness uses agents allow-lists
and handoffs.

00:07:30.969 --> 00:07:40.051
Also flag subagent instruction inheritance: never assume a subagent
silently read your root instructions—reinforce critical invariants

00:07:40.051 --> 00:07:43.236
like zero-packages in each worker's agent file!

00:07:43.967 --> 00:07:50.511
Move into Part Three: Hooks, Canvases, repository structure, and
subagent orchestration.

00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:59.870
These four slides complete the conceptual toolkit and show how
deterministic guardrails, visual workspaces, and hub-and-spoke

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.667
delegation fit together.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:11.067
Connect BOC's lifecycle table with a concrete dot-github-slash-hooks
JSON example.

00:08:11.067 --> 00:08:25.591
Unlike instructions—which ask an LLM nicely—hooks run deterministic
non-AI scripts at events like preToolUse, postToolUse, sessionStart,

00:08:25.591 --> 00:08:27.514
and subagentStart.

00:08:27.514 --> 00:08:42.045
A preToolUse script inspects the proposed tool JSON on stdin and can
return permissionDecision deny to block out-of-lane edits or secret

00:08:42.045 --> 00:08:42.793
access.

00:08:42.793 --> 00:08:53.191
Remind trainers: hooks execute code on the machine, so review hook
scripts and timeouts carefully!

00:08:53.900 --> 00:09:03.125
Explain Canvases clearly: instead of burying interactive artifacts
inside scrolling chat text, supported sessions in the GitHub Copilot

00:09:03.125 --> 00:09:11.667
App and VS Code open a side-by-side interactive surface—addressed by
canvasId for the type and instanceId for the live panel.

00:09:11.667 --> 00:09:20.712
Both the developer and the agent can inspect and drive actions on
that panel—like Microsoft Foundry's Agent Builder canvas or custom

00:09:20.712 --> 00:09:22.014
workspace canvases.

00:09:22.014 --> 00:09:29.025
Stress two caveats: opening a canvas doesn't replace running tests,
and secrets never belong on boards.

00:09:29.733 --> 00:09:38.452
Give attendees this exact blueprint on the left so they see how
instructions, agents, skills, and hooks coexist cleanly under

00:09:38.452 --> 00:09:39.220
dot-github.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:48.290
Then trace the subagent context diagram on the right: subagents are
stateless per invocation and do NOT see your main chat history!

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:56.984
That's a feature, not a bug—the Orchestrator must pass a
self-contained brief with allowed files and acceptance criteria, and

00:09:56.984 --> 00:10:04.147
the subagent absorbs thousands of tokens of file reads and test
output, returning only a crisp verdict.

00:10:04.867 --> 00:10:14.156
Here is the exact hub-and-spoke team learners build in Labs One and
Two, paired with v2's five collision-protection layers along the

00:10:14.156 --> 00:10:14.648
bottom!

00:10:14.648 --> 00:10:22.893
Point out why Chronicle Orchestrator only gets read, search, agent,
and todo—it structurally cannot write code itself!

00:10:22.893 --> 00:10:32.298
And emphasize that while subagents isolate context, parallel writers
in one checkout still collide—so combine worktree isolation, file

00:10:32.298 --> 00:10:38.264
partitioning, module seams, tool and hook enforcement, and human
integration testing.

00:10:38.967 --> 00:10:43.418
Presentation complete—now transition the room into hands-on
execution!

00:10:43.418 --> 00:10:52.117
From Slide Twenty-Four onward, keep the relevant lab slide projected
as a visual anchor while circulating the room and checking concrete

00:10:52.117 --> 00:10:54.419
pass-fail evidence at each boundary.

00:10:55.133 --> 00:10:58.217
Set the ground rules before starting the clock.

00:10:58.217 --> 00:11:07.187
Rule one: everyone must File Open Folder directly on workshop slash
Advanced slash sampleapp—if they open the repo root instead, VS Code

00:11:07.187 --> 00:11:10.353
won't discover the workspace agents they create!

00:11:10.353 --> 00:11:14.486
Rule two: Node twenty-two built-ins only—never run npm install.

00:11:14.486 --> 00:11:17.963
Rule three: verify every checkpoint before advancing.

00:11:18.667 --> 00:11:21.100
Start the eight-minute Lab Zero timer.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:29.798
Have learners run node dash-dash-version, node dash-dash-check src
slash server.js, and node dash-dash-test—expecting ten passing tests

00:11:29.798 --> 00:11:31.924
and zero failures out of the box.

00:11:31.924 --> 00:11:40.100
Verify they selected Agent mode on the Copilot harness with a
frontier model, and distribute the five Foundry Service Principal

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:44.413
parameters privately—never display client secrets on the projector!

00:11:45.133 --> 00:11:54.371
Give ten minutes for the four worker dot-agent-dot-md files, five
minutes for Chronicle Orchestrator, and two minutes to open Chat:

00:11:54.371 --> 00:11:57.897
Open Customizations and inspect all five profiles.

00:11:57.897 --> 00:12:07.355
Walk the room and check two things: Security must ONLY list read and
search, and Orchestrator must omit edit and execute while setting

00:12:07.355 --> 00:12:08.978
user-invocable to true.

00:12:08.978 --> 00:12:11.783
Fix any YAML indent warnings right away.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:21.343
In Lab Two, learners generate four SKILL.md playbooks, verify each
YAML name matches its folder name, and attach each skill to its

00:12:21.343 --> 00:12:24.132
specialist worker via skills frontmatter.

00:12:24.132 --> 00:12:33.170
At minute thirty-eight, have everyone open a fresh chat, type slash
to see their skills in the menu, and switch the agent dropdown to

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:37.995
Chronicle Orchestrator—from here on, they only prompt the
Orchestrator!

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:45.996
Pause for ninety seconds at the forty-minute mark to lock in the
foundation before hitting Azure APIs.

00:12:45.996 --> 00:12:51.360
Cold-call these three questions: Why didn't Orchestrator edit files
itself?

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.007
Because edit wasn't in its tool list!

00:12:54.007 --> 00:12:56.439
What loads at startup for a skill?

00:12:56.439 --> 00:12:58.299
Only name and description!

00:12:58.299 --> 00:13:01.660
And why hide workers with user-invocable false?

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:04.450
Clean UX with full subagent delegation!

00:13:05.167 --> 00:13:14.148
In Lab Three, learners send one prompt to Chronicle Orchestrator and
watch it dispatch Developer, Documentation, Security, and Tester!

00:13:14.148 --> 00:13:23.264
Highlight three coaching points: GET config returns hasClientSecret
boolean—never the secret; Tester validates Entra OAuth2 and Foundry

00:13:23.264 --> 00:13:31.974
responses against local node-http mock servers; and when testing
live credentials, separate Entra token timing from Foundry READY

00:13:31.974 --> 00:13:32.446
timing.

00:13:32.446 --> 00:13:36.803
If Azure is unreachable, mocked tests pass and mark live pending.

00:13:37.533 --> 00:13:41.065
Lab Four is the architectural payoff of the workshop!

00:13:41.065 --> 00:13:49.181
Orchestrator coordinates building buildSessionDigest and
chunkSessionDigest—stripping machine-specific workspace prefixes

00:13:49.181 --> 00:13:57.633
into portable relative paths, running single-pass synthesis for
short chats or map-reduce chunk digests for huge sessions, and

00:13:57.633 --> 00:14:01.188
wiring the live progress modal plus popup log viewer.

00:14:01.188 --> 00:14:09.783
Have learners inspect their generated summaries markdown file and
verify a teammate on another laptop could continue immediately!

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:14.017
Use Lab Five to prove disciplined scope control!

00:14:14.017 --> 00:14:23.145
Learners give Orchestrator a tight release brief with explicit
allowed files: generate the PNG and ICO via pure-Node zlib in

00:14:23.145 --> 00:14:29.623
render-icon.cjs, wire the favicon, pass release-icon.test.js, and
verify in the browser.

00:14:29.623 --> 00:14:39.190
Spend the final three minutes on a read-only tour of appsample's
Electron main and preload files—showing utility-process isolation

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:46.475
and why dot-env stays outside installers—without wasting class time
on gigabyte Electron downloads!

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:56.551
Close strong with concrete Monday-morning habits: trim bloated
copilot-instructions down to non-negotiables, turn one repeated team

00:14:56.551 --> 00:15:05.617
workflow into a SKILL.md with a sharp trigger description, create
one read-only security or code-review agent, add a preToolUse

00:15:05.617 --> 00:15:10.757
guardrail where mistakes hurt, and verify every handoff with real
tests.

00:15:10.757 --> 00:15:19.200
Point trainers and attendees to the lab guides, finished appsample
reference, and video kit—and open the floor for Q&A!
